# Airline Commercial Strategist — Independent Brief

**Run:** Quiet-by-Design Standards for MWAA Terminals
**Author lens:** 20 years airline commercial and network planning; 10 years airport-side. Sat in the rooms where hub-add and dehub decisions were made. Watched CVG, PIT, STL, MEM, CLE come off the network board.
**Central question I bring:** what will the carriers do in response? Any MWAA standard that does not model carrier behavior — signatory-carrier consent under the 2025 U&L, gate-operations impact at hub scale, and the CPE math a network planner actually runs — is incomplete on arrival.

---

## 1. Key Findings

1. **United at IAD is not a captive tenant.** United took ~72% of IAD enplanements in 2024 and executed a new long-term Dulles lease as part of the 2025 U&L cycle [Source: MWAA 2024 traffic statistics; MWAA press release, Aug 2024]. But United operates a second, larger East Coast hub 220 miles away at EWR (~46 million passengers 2024) [Source: United 2024 10-K]. Any standard that shifts marginal cost onto United-funded projects gets re-underwritten against EWR growth alternatives before it gets signed.
2. **American at DCA is captive by slot, not by loyalty.** American holds ~50% of DCA slots, its Washington hub, and picked up 2 of the 10 new beyond-perimeter exemptions in December 2024 [Source: DOT Order 2024-12-x, beyond-perimeter slot exemption award]. The slot regime protects American's DCA position more than any airport strategy could — but it also means American has no reason to concede on operational discretion. They will fight terminal standards that touch gate ops.
3. **MII is the veto that matters.** MWAA's 2025 U&L is a hybrid agreement with a $9.0B capital envelope pre-agreed by signatories. Standards adopted *after* the 2025 U&L that raise per-project cost above delegated thresholds trigger Majority-In-Interest review [Source: standard U.S. residual/hybrid U&L convention; specific MWAA MII thresholds not verified — flagged in airport context packet]. A "quiet" standard is cheap; a "quiet + acoustic-treated + STI-verified + BIM-modeled" standard bundled into every renovation is not, and that is where MII enters the room.
4. **CPE at IAD is already high and going higher.** MWAA IAD CPE was $28.34 in FY 2023 per operator disclosure [Source: MWAA CAFR / Airport rates and charges filing]. The expanded $20–22.5B Dulles program announced July 2026 layers more debt service onto a base that already sits above the U.S. large-hub median of roughly $15–20 [Source: FAA CATS/rates database; ACI-NA benchmarking]. Every incremental design mandate lands on top of that trajectory.
5. **The dehub warning signs are pattern, not prophecy.** CVG, PIT, STL, MEM, and CLE all featured (a) a single hub carrier with an alternative hub in the same region, (b) CPE trajectory into the mid-to-high teens or above, (c) a large just-completed capital program the carrier had signed off on but no longer needed after a merger. IAD hits (a) and partly (c) — Concourse E opens autumn 2026 and the $22B expansion is the next round. The critical differentiator is that DCA-area O&D demand and IAD's international gateway role remove the "there is no reason to be here" logic that killed the five dehubbed hubs.
6. **The quiet-by-design cost debate is the wrong debate at United's scale.** United spends roughly $250–350M annually at IAD in rates and charges [Source: back-of-envelope from MWAA airline-payments disclosures]. A $2–5M acoustic mandate per new concourse is invisible to that carrier's P&L. What is *not* invisible is a standard that (a) adds 30 seconds to boarding calls, (b) slows IROPS reaccommodation, or (c) forces gate agents to work around announcement restrictions. The threat vector is operational drag, not first-cost.
7. **Boarding intelligibility is a Part 139 and DOT-CR consumer-rule issue before it is an airline preference.** DOT's tarmac-delay and disability rules require effective communication in irregular operations [Source: 14 CFR Part 259; 14 CFR Part 382]. Any standard that reduces intelligibility — even briefly — is legally exposed. Airlines will invoke this to defeat aggressive "no PA" prescriptions and will be legally correct to do so.
8. **The MWAA leverage window is the 2025 U&L capital envelope, not the standard itself.** Standards adopted inside the $9.0B envelope are financeable and MII-covered. Standards proposed as add-ons after execution have to be re-negotiated. Timing determines whether the standard lands or dies.

