## 2. Evidence — What Airlines Actually Care About in Terminal Standards

### 2.1 The four-major oligopoly and MWAA's exposure to it

Post-consolidation U.S. airline structure is a four-major oligopoly (American, Delta, United, Southwest) with an ULCC fringe (Spirit, Frontier, Allegiant, plus the pending Spirit reorganization) [Source: DOT Form 41; carrier 10-Ks 2024]. MWAA sits at the concentrated end of that structure: United is dominant at IAD; American is dominant at DCA. Neither airport carries meaningful Southwest presence and DCA has zero ULCC service. This concentration is a double-edged sword — it removes the "diversification" argument for airport-favorable terms, and it means any standard becomes a bilateral negotiation with one carrier per airport in practice, whatever the U&L signatory list looks like on paper.

The 2008–2016 merger wave (Delta/Northwest 2008; United/Continental 2010; Southwest/AirTran 2011; American/US Airways 2013; Alaska/Virgin 2016) [Source: DOJ competitive filings and DOT orders for each] shifted leverage sharply toward carriers. Airports with a single dominant merged carrier lost the "let's play carriers against each other" tactic that had worked in the 1990s. MWAA's 2025 U&L reflects that world: 15-year term, pre-agreed capital envelope, hybrid rate-setting — an agreement carriers underwrite because the certainty helps them, not because MWAA had the whip hand.

### 2.2 Hub economics, banked operations, and the boarding-call cadence

A hub is not a marketing label; it is a banked operation that funnels connecting traffic into narrow arrival-departure windows to maximize connection combinations. United's IAD banking structure runs roughly 6–7 daily banks with 45–60 minute connection windows [Source: OAG schedules; author professional judgment]. Inside a bank, dozens of gates board simultaneously across concourses. Announcement discipline is not aesthetic — it is throughput. A missed boarding call at bank peak forces a rebook that cascades through the connecting itinerary. Missed-boarding rates and DOT on-time performance are the metrics network planners care about because they directly hit MCT (minimum connect time) marketing.

A "quiet" concourse that measurably slows boarding by even 10–20 seconds per gate at bank peak has real cost. Multiply across ~40 gates in Concourses C/D/E during a peak United bank and the number is not trivial. This is the operational metric MWAA must engage before proposing announcement caps, not after.

### 2.3 What separates a real hub from a large focus city — and why IAD is a real hub

United carried ~72% of IAD enplanements in 2024 [Source: MWAA CY2024 statistics]. International accounts for ~10.5M of IAD's 29M total passengers [Source: MWAA CY2025 traffic]. Star Alliance connections (Lufthansa, ANA, Turkish, others) hub or transit through IAD. This is a *real* hub — connecting complex, international gateway, single carrier dominance — not a focus city. Compare to Cleveland pre-2014 (Continental/United), where the O&D market was mid-sized and no international gateway function existed. That's why CLE was expendable and IAD is not.

### 2.4 Cost-per-enplanement — the number carriers actually track

CPE is the diagnostic. Rough current ranges at large U.S. hubs (FY 2023 filings, ACI-NA benchmarking):

- LAX ~$25–30 [Source: LAWA CAFR FY2023]
- JFK ~$35–40 pre-Terminal 6/1 completion [Source: PANYNJ statistical reports]
- ORD ~$28–32 post-OHare 21 [Source: CDA rates and charges]
- IAD ~$28.34 FY2023 [Source: MWAA airport rates and charges]
- ATL <$5–8 (Delta-dominated, residual, extraordinary throughput) [Source: HJAIA CAFR]
- MDW ~$12–15 [Source: CDA]
- MSP ~$12–16 [Source: MAC CAFR]

Where carriers begin to divert growth is not a single number; it is a *relative* threshold within the carrier's own network. United's IAD CPE compared to United's EWR CPE (roughly $30+) is the actual benchmark. As long as IAD remains within ~10–20% of the network median, United absorbs incremental cost. Above that band, the network planner starts modeling capacity redirects. The July 2026 $22B expansion, if funded predominantly through revenue debt paid via rates, will push IAD toward the top quartile — and that is the ceiling above which MII objections become real.

### 2.5 Use and lease agreement mechanics — where the leverage actually lives

Under a residual agreement, the airline pays whatever it takes to make the airport whole after non-airline revenue. Under a compensatory agreement, the airline pays only for direct cost recovery and the airport keeps upside. MWAA's 2025 U&L is a **hybrid** that pre-agreed a $9.0B capital envelope with airline consent inside it and left MWAA with more discretion on non-airline revenue than a pure residual would [Source: MWAA press release Aug 2024; MWAA U&L executive summary].

Two mechanisms matter for a design standard:

- **MII on capital additions:** Standard practice is that projects above a delegated threshold require signatory-airline consent. If a quiet-by-design mandate adds material cost to future projects, that cost falls under MII scrutiny project-by-project.
- **Change-order and rate reopening:** Standards adopted mid-agreement that materially raise operating cost can trigger rate re-computation. Airlines will not sign off on a standard that lacks a defined cost envelope.

The tactical implication: quiet-by-design lands more cleanly if it is codified in the MWAA Design Manual *inside* the 2025 U&L capital envelope, with per-project cost bands airlines can price into their MII posture. Retrofitting it as an add-on after the U&L was signed is a harder political sell.

### 2.6 Route economics: what makes a route survive or die

Network planners cut, upgauge, or hold based on a route P&L that includes station cost (ground handling, gate rent, terminal charges), fuel, crew, aircraft ownership, and marginal revenue net of dilution. Terminal-experience effects show up in the model only indirectly — via missed boardings, DOT on-time, and customer-satisfaction driving revenue premium (or discount). A quiet-terminal experience does *not* register as a revenue driver in the network planner's spreadsheet unless the carrier's brand team has assigned it a value, which none currently do at a level that would move a route decision.

This is important: the airport should not sell a quiet standard to airlines as "this will help you commercially." It won't, at the granularity carriers plan. It should be sold as (a) accessibility and disability-rule compliance, (b) an alignment with the airport's Master Plan trajectory to 90M passengers at IAD, and (c) an insurance policy against consumer-rule exposure in IROPS.

